To migrate your JSP + JavaScript-based web application that currently only works in Internet Explorer (IE) and fails in Microsoft Edge, you need to modernize your frontend to be compatible with modern browsers. IE is deprecated and unsupported by Microsoft, so migration is essential.

⸻

✅ Step-by-Step Migration Guide: From IE-only JSP+JS Web App to Modern Browsers (Edge, Chrome, Firefox, etc.)

⸻

🔍 Step 1: Understand Why It Only Works in IE

Start by identifying IE-specific code/features:
	•	Use of ActiveX, document.all, attachEvent, VBScript, X-UA-Compatible, etc.
	•	Reliance on IE-specific quirks mode or old DOM APIs
	•	Heavy use of inline scripts, old XMLHttpRequest code
	•	Old DOCTYPE or missing it (which forces quirks mode in IE)

⸻

🧹 Step 2: Clean Up and Modernize HTML/JS

🔁 Replace IE-Specific Features:
IE Feature
Replace With
document.all
document.querySelector
attachEvent()
addEventListener()
ActiveX controls
Standard HTML5 APIs (or server-side logic)
innerText
textContent
X-UA-Compatible tag
Remove it completely
Old DOM manipulation
Use modern JavaScript DOM APIs

🧪 Step 3: Test with Edge Developer Tools

Use F12 Developer Tools in Edge to:
	•	Open your app
	•	Check Console tab for JavaScript errors
	•	Check Network tab for failed requests (e.g., due to outdated APIs or incorrect MIME types)

⸻

🔄 Step 4: Refactor JavaScript
	•	Replace inline JavaScript with modular JS files
	•	Use modern syntax: let, const, arrow functions, template literals
	•	Consider using polyfills if targeting older versions of Edge
	•	Remove deprecated APIs like XMLDOM, ActiveXObject, etc.

⸻

🧰 Step 5: Update JSP to Use HTML5 Doctype

At the top of each JSP file:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">



